  "content": {
    "id": "p18-q5-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "5",
    "prompt": "Express each of the following numbers as a product of prime factors:",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p18-q5-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$2(2^8)+7(2^4)+6$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$2\\times3^2\\times5\\times7$\n\nFactorise as a quadratic in $2^4$, then evaluate the factors.",
          "worked": "Let $y=2^4=16$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}2y^2+7y+6&=2y^2+4y+3y+6\\\\2y^2+7y+6&=2y(y+2)+3(y+2)\\\\2y^2+7y+6&=(2y+3)(y+2)\\end{align*}$$\n\n$$\\begin{align*}2(2^8)+7(2^4)+6&=(2\\times16+3)(16+2)\\\\2(2^8)+7(2^4)+6&=35\\times18\\\\2(2^8)+7(2^4)+6&=(5\\times7)(2\\times3^2)\\\\2(2^8)+7(2^4)+6&=2\\times3^2\\times5\\times7\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 68
      },
      {
        "id": "p18-q5-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$6(2^{10})+2^5-2$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$2\\times3^2\\times7^3$\n\nFactorise as a quadratic in $2^5$, then evaluate the factors.",
          "worked": "Let $y=2^5=32$, so $2^{10}=y^2$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}6y^2+y-2&=6y^2+4y-3y-2\\\\6y^2+y-2&=2y(3y+2)-(3y+2)\\\\6y^2+y-2&=(3y+2)(2y-1)\\end{align*}$$\n\n$$\\begin{align*}6(2^{10})+2^5-2&=(3\\times32+2)(2\\times32-1)\\\\6(2^{10})+2^5-2&=98\\times63\\\\6(2^{10})+2^5-2&=(2\\times7^2)(3^2\\times7)\\\\6(2^{10})+2^5-2&=2\\times3^2\\times7^3\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 68
      },
      {
        "id": "p18-q5-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$8(3^6)+14(3^3)+3$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$3\\times19\\times109$\n\nFactorise as a quadratic in $3^3$, then evaluate the factors.",
          "worked": "Let $y=3^3=27$, so $3^6=y^2$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}8y^2+14y+3&=8y^2+12y+2y+3\\\\8y^2+14y+3&=4y(2y+3)+(2y+3)\\\\8y^2+14y+3&=(4y+1)(2y+3)\\end{align*}$$\n\n$$\\begin{align*}8(3^6)+14(3^3)+3&=(4\\times27+1)(2\\times27+3)\\\\8(3^6)+14(3^3)+3&=109\\times57\\\\8(3^6)+14(3^3)+3&=109\\times3\\times19\\\\8(3^6)+14(3^3)+3&=3\\times19\\times109\\end{align*}$$\n\n$109$ is prime: none of $2$, $3$, $5$ or $7$ divides it, and $\\sqrt{109}<11$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 84
      },
      {
        "id": "p18-q5-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$2(3^7)-3^3-1$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$2\\times41\\times53$\n\nUse $3^7=3(3^3)^2$, factorise the quadratic and evaluate its factors.",
          "worked": "Let $y=3^3=27$, so $3^7=3y^2$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}6y^2-y-1&=6y^2-3y+2y-1\\\\6y^2-y-1&=3y(2y-1)+(2y-1)\\\\6y^2-y-1&=(3y+1)(2y-1)\\end{align*}$$\n\n$$\\begin{align*}2(3^7)-3^3-1&=(3\\times27+1)(2\\times27-1)\\\\2(3^7)-3^3-1&=82\\times53\\\\2(3^7)-3^3-1&=(2\\times41)\\times53\\\\2(3^7)-3^3-1&=2\\times41\\times53\\end{align*}$$\n\n$41$ and $53$ are prime; testing primes up to their square roots confirms this.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 92
      }
    ]
  },
